      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Jim Shay is an American real estate professional and education supporter associated with Southborough, Massachusetts. A history graduate of Boston College, he served as president of Fay School's trustees. He and his wife, Monica, established university financial-aid funds, including a scholarship named for his father, businessman Joseph F. Shay Sr.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "An outgoing trustee's planned gift prompted Jim Shay and his wife, Monica, to make a similar commitment to Fay School in their wills. Shay had attended the school himself, and both daughters followed him there. The couple wanted their gift to help retain teachers and make the school accessible to pupils whose families could not otherwise afford it.\n\nHis community involvement also took a more public form. As a director of the South Middlesex Opportunity Council, Shay held annual overnight vigils in Southborough to draw attention to domestic violence. Beside his chair stood red silhouettes representing women killed locally, each carrying the victim's story for passers-by to read.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 87,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Primary donor interview and full contemporary local report support concrete giving decision and awareness activity."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 108,
    "html": "<p>An outgoing trustee's planned gift prompted Jim Shay and his wife, Monica, to make a similar commitment to Fay School in their wills. Shay had attended the school himself, and both daughters followed him there. The couple wanted their gift to help retain teachers and make the school accessible to pupils whose families could not otherwise afford it.</p>\r\n<p>His community involvement also took a more public form. As a director of the South Middlesex Opportunity Council, Shay held annual overnight vigils in Southborough to draw attention to domestic violence. Beside his chair stood red silhouettes representing women killed locally, each carrying the victim's story for passers-by to read.</p>\r\n"
  },
